  "nameStory": "The Bleeding Fairy Helmet gets its dramatic name from its most striking feature - when you cut or break the stem, it 'bleeds' dark red drops of liquid called latex. This crimson ooze makes it look like a tiny injured fairy's cap, earning it the wonderfully spooky common name that perfectly captures its most memorable characteristic.",
  "description": "The Bleeding Fairy Helmet is a small, delicate mushroom with a reddish-brown cap that reaches up to about 1.5 inches wide. It has a thin, fragile stem covered in coarse hairs at the base and whitish gills underneath the cap. What makes this little mushroom truly special is its party trick - break it open and it bleeds dark red liquid like a tiny wounded creature. You'll typically find these charming fungi growing in small clusters on rotting deciduous logs and stumps.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bleeding",
      "description": "When cut or broken, the stem and cap ooze dark red latex that looks exactly like blood - this is the key identifying feature that gives the mushroom its name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Reddish-brown cap up to 1.5 inches wide with distinctively scalloped or wavy edges that feel delicate and papery to the touch",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Thin, fragile reddish-brown stem with a cluster of thick, coarse hairs at the very base that feel rough when rubbed between your fingers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Whitish gills that contrast nicely with the reddish cap and may show reddish staining where damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Always grows in small groups or clusters on decaying wood, never alone, and prefers hardwood logs and stumps over coniferous wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Bleeding Fairy Helmets on rotting logs, stumps, and fallen trunks of deciduous trees throughout New England's forests, with a particular fondness for beech wood. They thrive in moist, shaded woodland areas where decomposing hardwood provides the nutrients they need. You'll often spot them in mixed forests and along woodland trails where fallen trees are slowly returning to the soil.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early mushrooms may appear on logs as the weather warms and spring rains provide moisture, though this isn't their peak season.",
    "summer": "Active fruiting during humid summer months, especially after periods of rain when the rotting logs they inhabit stay consistently moist.",
    "fall": "Peak season for fruiting bodies, when cool temperatures and frequent rainfall create ideal conditions. This is when you're most likely to find fresh clusters.",
    "winter": "Mostly absent in New England winters, though old dried specimens may persist on logs until covered by snow."
  },
  "funFact": "Both the mushroom and its underground fungal threads (mycelia) actually glow in the dark with a faint green light - they're naturally bioluminescent! While the glow is too weak to see with the naked eye in most conditions, sensitive cameras can capture this otherworldly fairy light emanating from rotting logs.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other small brown Mycena species, but the dark red bleeding when broken is unique - no other common New England mushroom produces this dramatic red latex. Remember: red bleeds = Bleeding Fairy Helmet.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The scientific name 'haematopus' literally means 'blood foot' in Greek, referring to the red latex that appears when the stem is broken. Despite being called a 'fairy helmet,' it's actually classified in the bonnet group, and you may see it referred to as the Burgundydrop Bonnet in some field guides.",
  "compareHints": "Among its many Mycena relatives in New England, the Bleeding Fairy Helmet is unique for its red latex - most other bonnets like the Common Bonnet or Milking Bonnet produce white or clear liquids when broken. The Bleeding Bonnet (Mycena sanguinolenta) also bleeds red but grows from soil rather than wood and lacks the distinctive hairy stem base.",
